From 7d42081de3ba3f83d205a1318b7e67a01cd0d22e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ashfordeOU <236818772+ashfordeOU@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 13:00:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] C7 step 1: kshana ephem-export writes a JPL Horizons state
 table as a CCSDS OEM, a CCSDS OPM or an SPK kernel (type 13 or 9) and reads
 it back

src/ephem_export.rs reads a Horizons vector table (KM-S, TDB; object, centre, NAIF ids,
frame and request time from the header) and writes the OEM 2.0 (every state), the OPM 3.0
(one state) or an SPK segment through spk_writer. Values and epochs are copied, not
computed: doubles in shortest round-trip form, calendar epochs as written, SPK epochs as
exact TDB seconds past J2000; ICRF is SPK frame code 1. read_back compares every state
read back by the engine's own readers (OEM data lines and parse_oem, OpmMessage::parse,
the SPK's stored nodes and its interpolation at each node).

On the CAPSTONE fixture (4,321 states) every state reads back with the same bits from the
OEM and the SPK, and the OPM's state too; no epoch differs. Checked once outside the repo
with CSPICE N0067 (spiceypy): the SPK's records and epochs are the same bits, spkezr at the
nodes within 1.5e-11 km.

Surfaces: CLI ephem-export, surface::ephem_export, Python, WebAssembly and the MCP tool
ephem_export (53 tools). Every surface writes the same bytes: the SHA-256 of the three
CAPSTONE files are pinned in tests/fixtures/capstone_horizons/ephem_export.sha256 and
checked by tests/ephem_export_capstone.rs, the Python binding test and the MCP round trip.
ReadSegment::nodes exposes a type 9/13 segment's stored epochs and states.

Docs: docs/FLIGHT-DYNAMICS-EXPORT.md, COMMANDS and SURFACES rows, the README and MCP
README tool tables, CHANGELOG. i18n: the new engine messages and the usage line, with
their templates; allowances for the CCSDS keywords and the report's JSON keys.
